//! vm — a process's memory beyond its ELF image: the break, anonymous
//! mappings (px12, M-PX3), and since px62 mappings of files and shared
//! memory, `mremap` and `msync`.
//!
//! Written from man-pages `brk(2)`, `mmap(2)`, `munmap(2)`,
//! `mprotect(2)`, the uapi `asm-generic/mman-common.h` and
//! `linux/mman.h` (the PROT_* and MAP_* numbers), and black-box strace
//! of glibc-static programs on Linux (what they ask for and what Linux
//! answers); px62: `mremap(2)`, `msync(2)`, `madvise(2)`, `fork(2)`'s
//! and `execve(2)`'s words on mappings, `truncate(2)`, POSIX.1-2024's
//! `mmap()`, `msync()` and `munmap()`, the uapi `linux/mman.h`
//! (MREMAP_*) and `asm-generic/mman-common.h` (MS_*), and a static
//! probe's answers on Linux (user/elf/maps.c, tests/mpx3-maps). No Linux
//! source; no other kernel's source.
//!
//! **The words** live in the process's area (kernel/sched's
//! `proc_area`, zero for a fresh process): the break's start and its
//! current value, and the mmap cursor.
//!
//! **The break** starts at the page above the highest byte of the ELF
//! image (`start`; Linux places a static program's there too, plus a
//! random offset PAX does not model). `brk(addr)` answers the current
//! break when addr is 0, below the start, or past BRK_MAX (64 MiB) above
//! it, or when a page it would need is already mapped (as Linux answers
//! a refused `brk` with the old break); otherwise it maps zeroed frames
//! (read-write, no-execute, user) for every page up to `addr`'s, or
//! unmaps and frees those above, and answers `addr`.
//!
//! **mmap** of anonymous private memory: a fresh zeroed frame per
//! page, allocated at the call (no demand paging), with the leaf flags
//! PROT_* asks for (PROT_WRITE adds RW, no PROT_EXEC is NX, PROT_NONE
//! takes U/S away so ring 3 cannot touch the page while its frame stays
//! counted). Without MAP_FIXED the address is chosen top-down from
//! MMAP_TOP (0x7f0000000000, below which kernel/elf loads nothing and
//! above which the stack lives), the cursor only ever moving down, so no
//! address is handed out twice in a process's life and a hint is
//! ignored; MAP_FIXED replaces whatever is mapped there, and
//! MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE answers -EEXIST instead. A length of 0 or a
//! fixed address off a page is -EINVAL, an address range outside the
//! user half -ENOMEM, no frames -ENOMEM (what was mapped given back).
//! `munmap` frees every mapped page of a range (holes are fine, as on
//! Linux); `mprotect` rewrites the leaves of a range every page of which
//! is mapped (-ENOMEM otherwise). (Until px62 a mapping of a file was
//! refused by name, -ENODEV, and MAP_SHARED -EINVAL.)
//!
//! **Files and shared memory** (px62, pax#128). PAX has kept no record
//! of a mapping beyond its page tables and the reservations below; a
//! mapping of a file needs one, since its pages are filled when they are
//! touched. A **record** (192 a process, five words each, after the
//! reservations in the area) holds a range, the file (kernel/tree's
//! inode, held while the record lives: `tree.hold`), the file offset of
//! the range's first byte, the protection, whether it is shared, and
//! whether a shared one may be made writable (its descriptor was open
//! for writing and not for appending). There is no page cache to write:
//! kernel/tree keeps a file's bytes in frames of its own and nowhere
//! else, so those frames are the cache (R-px62-1). A page of a record is
//! filled at its first touch (`fault`, from kernel/user's page-fault path
//! and kernel/uaccess before it checks a buffer), never at the call:
//! - a shared mapping maps the file's frame itself (`tree.page`: a page
//!   still the initramfs's, or a hole, gets its frame then, as a first
//!   `write` would give it), read-write when PROT_WRITE asks; so the
//!   mapping, a second mapping, a forked child's copy and `read` and
//!   `write` of the file are one set of bytes, and `msync` has nothing
//!   to write;
//! - a private mapping maps the file's frame read-only, COW when it may
//!   be written; the first write copies the page into a frame of the
//!   process's own (`unshare`), and a write fault on a page not touched
//!   yet copies at once (`tree.read_page`, no frame made in the tree).
//!   So a private page not yet written sees a later `write(2)` of the
//!   file and a written one does not, as on Linux (POSIX leaves it
//!   unspecified). With the tree at its cap a read fault copies too.
//! A page whose offset is at or past the file's end is SIGBUS (BUS_ADRERR,
//! kernel/user and kernel/signal), the bytes after the end in the last
//! page read zero; a file cut by `truncate` is unmapped past its new end
//! in every process first (`cut_maps`: copied private pages too, as
//! Linux unmaps them, measured), so its frames can go back. Such a leaf
//! carries kernel/paging's BORROWED bit: `munmap`, `free_user` and every
//! unmap here leave the frame to the file, and a fork's copy maps it
//! again. MAP_SHARED|MAP_ANONYMOUS, and MAP_SHARED of /dev/zero, is a
//! record of an inode with no name (`tree.anon`) as long as the mapping,
//! whose frames go back with its last record; MAP_PRIVATE of /dev/zero is
//! anonymous private memory. `mprotect` splits a record at the range's
//! ends and rewrites its pages' leaves (-EACCES for PROT_WRITE on a
//! shared mapping that may not be made writable); `munmap` and MAP_FIXED
//! cut records as reservations are cut (-ENOMEM when a cut needs a
//! record and none is free); MADV_DONTNEED of a record's page unmaps it,
//! so its next touch reads the file again. A fork's child holds every
//! record's file again (`inherit`); `execve` and the end of a process let
//! them go (`drop_all`). Refused by name: a file or shared mapping in a
//! vfork child (its space is its parent's), and records full (-ENOMEM
//! each).
//!
//! **mremap** (px62, pax#127; `mremap(2)`): the old range must be one
//! mapping — all of it inside one record, or inside one reservation, or
//! present anonymous pages with one leaf's flags — else -EFAULT (two
//! protections, a hole, the end of a mapping crossed). Sizes are rounded
//! up to pages; the same size answers the old address, a smaller one
//! unmaps the tail in place. A larger one grows in place when every page
//! after the old range up to the new size is free (anonymous pages get
//! zeroed frames, a reservation or a record its range extended), else
//! with MREMAP_MAYMOVE moves to a range `pick` chooses: the pages' leaves
//! move (no byte is copied), a reservation or a record moves with them,
//! and what was the old range is unmapped; without it -ENOMEM. A flag
//! that is none, an address off a page, a new size of 0, MREMAP_FIXED or
//! MREMAP_DONTUNMAP without MREMAP_MAYMOVE, and an old size of 0 are
//! -EINVAL, Linux's own answers. Refused by name with -EINVAL (R-px62-2,
//! the answer of a kernel that has `mremap` but not the flag; -ENOSYS
//! would say there is no `mremap` at all): MREMAP_FIXED with
//! MREMAP_MAYMOVE, MREMAP_DONTUNMAP with it, and an old size of 0 of a
//! shared mapping (Linux makes a second mapping of the same pages).
//! The process's served calls, how many grew in place and how many moved
//! are kept for kernel/user's line at its end.
//!
//! **msync** (px62): MS_ASYNC, MS_SYNC and MS_INVALIDATE are 0 on any
//! range every page of which is mapped (the file's frames are the file);
//! -EINVAL for a flag that is none, MS_ASYNC with MS_SYNC, or an address
//! off a page; -ENOMEM for a page that is not mapped; a length of 0 is 0.
//!
//! **Reservations** (px26). A mapping made with PROT_NONE takes no
//! frame: its range is kept in the area (sixteen ranges a process) and
//! its pages stay unmapped, so a touch faults, as PROT_NONE must. glibc
//! reserves this way and backs what it uses with `mprotect`: a thread's
//! stack (2 MiB and a guard page) and a thread's own heap (128 MiB, of
//! which it backs 132 KiB at first), measured on pelt's second thread.
//! `mprotect` to a protection with any access backs each reserved page
//! it covers with a zeroed frame; `munmap` and a MAP_FIXED mapping take
//! their range out of the reservations (a hole cut in the middle of one
//! needs a second entry: when none is free the tail stays reserved,
//! address space lost and nothing else). A reserved address is never
//! handed out by a later `mmap`. With every entry in use (px34: 256; sixteen before) a
//! PROT_NONE mapping is made the old way, a frame a page. A fork's
//! child inherits the reservations with the area; `execve` drops them.
//!
//! **Backed on touch** (px34: `MAP_NORESERVE`, what wolf's runtime maps
//! a task's 8 MiB stack with). A private anonymous read-write mapping
//! with MAP_NORESERVE takes no frame at the call: its range is kept as
//! a reservation of its own kind, and a page of it is backed with a
//! zeroed read-write frame the first time ring 3 touches it (`back`,
//! from kernel/user's page-fault path) or a system call's buffer covers
//! it (kernel/uaccess backs before it checks). `mprotect` to PROT_NONE of
//! part of one (the runtime's 64 KiB guard) makes that part an ordinary
//! reservation: it faults for good; to read-write it changes nothing;
//! to anything else it backs the pages now. MADV_DONTNEED and MADV_FREE
//! on a page of one give its frame back: the next touch reads zeros,
//! which both allow (`madvise(2)`). Without MAP_NORESERVE a mapping is
//! backed at the call, as before. The reservations (both kinds) are 256
//! a process (px34; 16 before), after the descriptors in the area.
//!
//! **madvise** (28, px26): MADV_DONTNEED on private anonymous memory
//! makes the pages read zero again (`madvise(2)`); PAX zeroes them in
//! place. MADV_GUARD_INSTALL (102, Linux 6.13, which a newer glibc asks
//! for below a thread's stack) is honoured, not taken as advice: the
//! pages lose user access, so a touch faults, which is all a guard is
//! for; a reserved page already faults. MADV_GUARD_REMOVE (103) gives
//! access back, read-write and no-execute: PAX keeps no protection of a
//! page beside its leaf, and a guard sits in a stack. MADV_NORMAL,
//! RANDOM, SEQUENTIAL, WILLNEED, FREE, HUGEPAGE, NOHUGEPAGE, DONTDUMP
//! and DODUMP are hints with nothing to do: 0. Any other advice is
//! refused by name, -EINVAL. A range with a page neither mapped nor
//! reserved is -ENOMEM.
//!
//! Everything is in the user half, so the process's death gives it all
//! back with its space (kernel/paging's `free_user`). A refusal's name
//! is left in the area's REFUSAL word for kernel/user to log. No region,
//! no heap.

use frames
use paging
use sched
use tree

const PAGE: u64 = 4096
const BRK_MAX: u64 = 0x4000000 // 64 MiB
const MMAP_TOP: u64 = 0x00007f0000000000
const LOW: u64 = 0x10000 // 64 KiB: Linux's default vm.mmap_min_addr

// The area's words (kernel/files and kernel/user use others).
const A_BRK_START: u64 = 0
const A_BRK: u64 = 1
const A_MMAP: u64 = 2
const A_REFUSAL: u64 = 3
const A_RESV: u64 = 4608 // px34: 256 reserved ranges, (lo, hi) each, after kernel/files' 1024 descriptors (px26: sixteen at word 222); lo 0 a free entry; hi's bit 0 set for a range backed on touch
const RESV: u64 = 256
const LAZY: u64 = 1 // in hi: the range is backed on touch (MAP_NORESERVE)
// px62: the records of a file's mapping (or shared memory's), after the
// reservations (kernel/sched's area grew to 49152 bytes for them).
const A_MAPS: u64 = 5120 // MAPS records of M_WORDS words; lo 0 a free record
const MAPS: u64 = 192
const M_WORDS: u64 = 5
const M_LO: u64 = 0 // the range's first byte
const M_HI: u64 = 1 // the first byte past it
const M_INO: u64 = 2 // the file (kernel/tree's inode)
const M_OFF: u64 = 3 // the file offset of the range's first byte
const M_FL: u64 = 4 // the protection (PROT_*), M_SHARED, M_MAYWRITE
const M_SHARED: u64 = 0x100
const M_MAYWRITE: u64 = 0x200 // a shared mapping that may be made writable
const A_REMAP: u64 = 6080 // px62: mremap's census: calls served, grown in place, moved
const A_MAPS_TOP: u64 = 6083 // px62: one past the highest record made since the last `drop_all` (0: none), so a process with no record pays nothing per page
const ADDR: u64 = 0x000ffffffffff000 // a leaf's frame (kernel/paging's)
/// px62: `fault`'s answers: the program's to die of (SIGSEGV), backed
/// (the access runs again), a page past its file's end (SIGBUS).
pub const F_NO: u64 = 0
pub const F_OK: u64 = 1
pub const F_BUS: u64 = 2
/// px62: what kernel/files says a descriptor allows a mapping (its
/// `map_target`): read, write, append.
pub const ACC_READ: u64 = 1
pub const ACC_WRITE: u64 = 2
pub const ACC_APPEND: u64 = 4
const MREMAP_MAYMOVE: u64 = 1
const MREMAP_FIXED: u64 = 2
const MREMAP_DONTUNMAP: u64 = 4
const MS_ASYNC: u64 = 1
const MS_INVALIDATE: u64 = 2
const MS_SYNC: u64 = 4
const MAP_SYNC: u64 = 0x80000

const PROT_WRITE: u64 = 0x2
const PROT_EXEC: u64 = 0x4
const PROT_KNOWN: u64 = 0x7 // READ | WRITE | EXEC
const PROT_GROWS: u64 = 0x03000000 // PROT_GROWSDOWN | PROT_GROWSUP: accepted, ignored
const PROT_BAD: u64 = 0xfcfffff8 // every other bit of the 32-bit int (-EINVAL)
const MAP_TYPE: u64 = 0x0f
const MAP_SHARED: u64 = 0x01
const MAP_PRIVATE: u64 = 0x02
const MAP_SHARED_VALIDATE: u64 = 0x03
const MAP_FIXED: u64 = 0x10
const MAP_ANONYMOUS: u64 = 0x20
const MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE: u64 = 0x100000
const MAP_NORESERVE: u64 = 0x4000 // px34
const PROT_RW: u64 = 0x3

// -errno as %rax carries it (uapi asm-generic/errno-base.h).
const NEG_ENOMEM: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffff4 // -12
const NEG_EEXIST: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffef // -17
const NEG_ENODEV: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffed // -19
const NEG_EINVAL: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffea // -22
const NEG_EFAULT: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffff2 // -14 (px62)
const NEG_EACCES: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffff3 // -13 (px62)
const NEG_EOVERFLOW: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffb5 // -75 (px62)
const NEG_EOPNOTSUPP: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffa1 // -95 (px62)

// Refusal codes (the REFUSAL word), named by `why`. 1 and 2 were a
// file's mapping and MAP_SHARED, refused until px62.
const R_NO_FRAMES: u64 = 3
const R_ADVICE: u64 = 4 // px26
const R_RECORDS: u64 = 5 // px62
const R_VFORK: u64 = 6 // px62
const R_REMAP_FIXED: u64 = 7 // px62
const R_DONTUNMAP: u64 = 8 // px62
const R_DUP: u64 = 9 // px62

const NEG_EAGAIN: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffff5 // -11
const PROT_NONE_BITS: u64 = 0x7
const MADV_DONTNEED: u64 = 4
const MADV_FREE: u64 = 8 // px34
const MADV_GUARD_INSTALL: u64 = 102
const MADV_GUARD_REMOVE: u64 = 103

fn get(a: u64, i: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: `a` is a process area (sched.proc_area), 49152 bytes; i < 6144.
    unsafe { ((a + i * 8) as *u64).read_volatile() }
}

fn put(a: u64, i: u64, v: u64) {
    // # Safety: as `get`.
    unsafe { ((a + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(v) }
}

fn page_up(x: u64) -> u64 {
    (x + PAGE - 1) & !(PAGE - 1)
}

/// The refusal the last call left (0 none), cleared by reading it.
pub fn take_refusal(a: u64) -> u64 {
    let r = get(a, A_REFUSAL)
    put(a, A_REFUSAL, 0)
    r
}

/// A refusal code's name.
pub fn why(r: u64) -> str {
    if r == R_RECORDS {
        return "mapping records full (192 a process)"
    }
    if r == R_VFORK {
        return "a file or shared mapping in a vfork child (its space is its parent's)"
    }
    if r == R_REMAP_FIXED {
        return "MREMAP_FIXED (PAX moves a mapping only to where it chooses)"
    }
    if r == R_DONTUNMAP {
        return "MREMAP_DONTUNMAP (PAX keeps no emptied old range)"
    }
    if r == R_DUP {
        return "mremap of an old size of 0 (a second mapping of shared pages)"
    }
    if r == R_NO_FRAMES {
        return "out of frames"
    }
    if r == R_ADVICE {
        return "madvise advice PAX does not know"
    }
    ""
}

// --- reservations (px26) -------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether user address `va` lies in a reserved range (PROT_NONE, or
/// px34's backed on touch), or (px62) in a record: a fault there is an
/// access the mapping refuses (SEGV_ACCERR), not a hole.
pub fn reserved(a: u64, va: u64) -> bool {
    reserved_from(a, va) != 0 || map_at(a, va & !(PAGE - 1)) != MAPS
}

fn hi_of(a: u64, i: u64) -> u64 {
    get(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1) & !LAZY
}

fn lazy_of(a: u64, i: u64) -> u64 {
    get(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1) & LAZY
}

/// Reserve [lo, hi), backed on touch when `lazy` is LAZY: false when no
/// entry is free.
fn reserve_kind(a: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64, lazy: u64) -> bool {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV {
        if get(a, A_RESV + i * 2) == 0 {
            put(a, A_RESV + i * 2, lo)
            put(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1, hi | lazy)
            return true
        }
        i += 1
    }
    false
}

/// Reserve [lo, hi) (PROT_NONE): false when no entry is free.
fn reserve(a: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64) -> bool {
    reserve_kind(a, lo, hi, 0)
}

/// Take [lo, hi) out of every reservation: an entry inside it goes, one
/// that overlaps an end is cut back, one that contains it is split in
/// two (or, with no free entry, keeps its tail too). A cut keeps its
/// entry's kind.
fn unreserve(a: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64) {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV {
        let rl = get(a, A_RESV + i * 2)
        let rh = hi_of(a, i)
        let k = lazy_of(a, i)
        if rl != 0 && lo < rh && hi > rl {
            if lo <= rl && hi >= rh {
                put(a, A_RESV + i * 2, 0)
                put(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1, 0)
            } else if lo <= rl {
                put(a, A_RESV + i * 2, hi)
            } else if hi >= rh {
                put(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1, lo | k)
            } else {
                put(a, A_RESV + i * 2 + 1, lo | k)
                let _kept = reserve_kind(a, hi, rh, k)
            }
        }
        i += 1
    }
}

/// px34: whether user page `pg` lies in a range backed on touch.
fn lazy_at(a: u64, pg: u64) -> bool {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV {
        let lo = get(a, A_RESV + i * 2)
        if lo != 0 && pg >= lo && pg < hi_of(a, i) {
            return lazy_of(a, i) != 0
        }
        i += 1
    }
    false
}

/// px34: back user page `pg` of the space at `pml4` with a zeroed
/// read-write frame when it lies in a range backed on touch and has none
/// yet: true when it did (px62: `fault`'s, for kernel/user's page-fault
/// path and kernel/uaccess before it checks a buffer). False otherwise,
/// or with no frame left.
fn back(a: u64, pml4: u64, pg: u64) -> bool {
    if pg >= paging.USER_TOP || mapped(pml4, pg) != 0 || !lazy_at(a, pg) {
        return false
    }
    map_zero(pml4, pg, paging.RW | paging.NX)
}

/// Whether user page `pg` is taken: mapped, or reserved, or (px62) in
/// a record.
fn taken(a: u64, pml4: u64, pg: u64) -> bool {
    mapped(pml4, pg) != 0 || reserved(a, pg)
}

/// When `pg` is in a reservation: the first address past the run of
/// reserved pages it is in, else 0 (so a search can step over a whole
/// reservation, not page by page: a thread's heap reserves 128 MiB).
fn reserved_from(a: u64, pg: u64) -> u64 {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV {
        let lo = get(a, A_RESV + i * 2)
        if lo != 0 && pg >= lo && pg < hi_of(a, i) {
            return lo
        }
        i += 1
    }
    0
}

/// The break of a process whose ELF image ends at `top` (its highest
/// PT_LOAD byte + 1): the page above it; the mmap cursor at its start
/// (px14: `execve` calls this too, on an area the old program used).
pub fn start(a: u64, top: u64) {
    put(a, A_BRK_START, page_up(top))
    put(a, A_BRK, page_up(top))
    put(a, A_MMAP, 0) // px14: execve starts the cursor afresh
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV * 2 {
        put(a, A_RESV + i, 0) // px26: and with no reservation
        i += 1
    }
    drop_all(a) // px62: and no record (`execve` lets the old program's files go)
}

/// The current break (0 before `start`).
pub fn current_break(a: u64) -> u64 {
    get(a, A_BRK)
}

/// The frame mapped at user page `va`, or 0.
fn mapped(pml4: u64, va: u64) -> u64 {
    paging.translate(pml4, va) else 0
}

/// Map a zeroed frame at user page `va` with `flags` (RW, NX; U/S and P
/// added). False when no frame or table is left (nothing mapped).
fn map_zero(pml4: u64, va: u64, flags: u64) -> bool {
    let pa = frames.alloc() else 0
    if pa == 0 {
        return false
    }
    let v = pa + frames.hhdm()
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < PAGE / 8 {
        // # Safety: the frame just taken, through the HHDM.
        unsafe { ((v + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(0) }
        i += 1
    }
    let r = paging.map_user(pml4, va, pa, flags) else 99
    if r == 99 {
        frames.free(pa)
        return false
    }
    true
}

/// Unmap and free every mapped page of [lo, hi) (page-aligned); px62:
/// a borrowed page's frame is its file's, unmapped and left.
fn unmap_range(pml4: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64) {
    var pg = lo
    while pg < hi {
        drop_page(pml4, pg)
        pg += PAGE
    }
}

/// px62: unmap user page `pg`, freeing its frame unless it is borrowed
/// (a file's page).
fn drop_page(pml4: u64, pg: u64) {
    let e = paging.leaf(pml4, pg)
    if e == 0 {
        return
    }
    let pa = paging.unmap(pml4, pg) else 0
    if pa != 0 && e & paging.BORROWED == 0 {
        frames.free(pa)
    }
}

/// `brk(addr)`: the new break, or the old one when refused.
pub fn brk(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64) -> u64 {
    let lo = get(a, A_BRK_START)
    let cur = get(a, A_BRK)
    if lo == 0 || addr < lo || addr - lo > BRK_MAX {
        return cur
    }
    let have = page_up(cur)
    let want = page_up(addr)
    if want > have {
        var pg = have
        while pg < want {
            if taken(a, pml4, pg) { // px62: a reservation or a record too
                return cur
            }
            pg += PAGE
        }
        pg = have
        while pg < want {
            if !map_zero(pml4, pg, paging.RW | paging.NX) {
                unmap_range(pml4, have, pg)
                return cur
            }
            pg += PAGE
        }
    } else if want < have {
        unmap_range(pml4, want, have)
    }
    put(a, A_BRK, addr)
    addr
}

/// The leaf flags for `prot` (P and U/S are map_user's): RW for
/// PROT_WRITE, NX without PROT_EXEC.
fn leaf(prot: u64) -> u64 {
    var f: u64 = 0
    if prot & PROT_WRITE != 0 {
        f = f | paging.RW
    }
    if prot & PROT_EXEC == 0 {
        f = f | paging.NX
    }
    f
}

/// The lowest address of a free run of `n` bytes (page multiple) at or
/// below the cursor; 0 when none is left above LOW.
fn pick(a: u64, pml4: u64, n: u64) -> u64 {
    var top = get(a, A_MMAP)
    if top == 0 {
        top = MMAP_TOP
    }
    while top >= LOW + n {
        let lo = top - n
        var pg = top
        var clash: u64 = 0
        while pg > lo && clash == 0 {
            pg -= PAGE
            if mapped(pml4, pg) != 0 {
                clash = pg + 1
            } else {
                let rl = reserved_from(a, pg) // px26
                if rl != 0 {
                    clash = rl + 1 // below the whole reservation
                } else {
                    let r = map_at(a, pg) // px62: below the whole record
                    if r != MAPS {
                        clash = mw(a, r, M_LO) + 1
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if clash == 0 {
            put(a, A_MMAP, lo)
            return lo
        }
        top = clash - 1 // below the mapped page
    }
    0
}

/// px62: where a new mapping of `n` bytes (a page multiple) goes:
/// MAP_FIXED's address (what was there unmapped first, reservations and
/// records cut), MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE's (-EEXIST when anything is there),
/// else `pick`'s; -errno. `extra` records the caller will make must be
/// free beside those a cut needs (-ENOMEM, nothing changed).
fn place(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, n: u64, flags: u64, extra: u64) -> u64 {
    if flags & (MAP_FIXED | MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE) != 0 {
        if addr % PAGE != 0 {
            return NEG_EINVAL
        }
        if addr < LOW || addr >= paging.USER_TOP || n > paging.USER_TOP - addr {
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
        var pg = addr
        while pg < addr + n {
            if taken(a, pml4, pg) && flags & MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE != 0 && flags & MAP_FIXED == 0 {
                return NEG_EEXIST
            }
            pg += PAGE
        }
        if !splits_ok(a, addr, addr + n, extra) {
            put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_RECORDS)
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
        unmap_range(pml4, addr, addr + n)
        unreserve(a, addr, addr + n) // px26
        unmap_records(a, addr, addr + n) // px62
        return addr
    }
    if free_records(a) < extra {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_RECORDS)
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    let va = pick(a, pml4, n)
    if va == 0 {
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    va
}

/// `mmap(addr, len, prot, flags, fd, off)` of anonymous memory (px62:
/// kernel/user sends a file's here only for /dev/zero, as anonymous):
/// the address, or -errno. MAP_SHARED is a record of an inode with no
/// name (px62, `shared_anon`).
pub fn mmap(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, len: u64, prot: u64, flags: u64) -> u64 {
    let kind = flags & MAP_TYPE
    if (kind != MAP_PRIVATE && kind != MAP_SHARED && kind != MAP_SHARED_VALIDATE) || len == 0 || prot & PROT_BAD != 0 {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if kind == MAP_SHARED_VALIDATE && flags & MAP_SYNC != 0 {
        return NEG_EOPNOTSUPP // px62: no file here keeps its bytes in memory a CPU writes straight to (MAP_SYNC's DAX)
    }
    if len > paging.USER_TOP {
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    let n = page_up(len)
    if kind != MAP_PRIVATE {
        return shared_anon(a, pml4, addr, n, prot, flags)
    }
    let va = place(a, pml4, addr, n, flags, 0)
    if failed(va) {
        return va
    }
    if prot & PROT_KNOWN == 0 && reserve(a, va, va + n) {
        return va // px26: PROT_NONE takes no frame; `mprotect` backs what is used
    }
    if flags & MAP_NORESERVE != 0 && prot & PROT_KNOWN == PROT_RW && reserve_kind(a, va, va + n, LAZY) {
        return va // px34: backed page by page where it is touched
    }
    let fl = leaf(prot)
    var pg = va
    while pg < va + n {
        if !map_zero(pml4, pg, fl) {
            unmap_range(pml4, va, pg)
            put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_NO_FRAMES)
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
        if prot & PROT_KNOWN == 0 {
            let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, fl) // PROT_NONE: no U/S
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    va
}

/// Whether `r` is an -errno.
fn failed(r: u64) -> bool {
    r >= 0xfffffffffffff001
}

/// px62: MAP_SHARED|MAP_ANONYMOUS (and MAP_SHARED of /dev/zero) of `n`
/// bytes: a record of a new inode with no name of `n` bytes
/// (`tree.anon`), read-write when PROT_WRITE asks, filled at its first
/// touch with the inode's frames, which a fork's child shares.
fn shared_anon(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, n: u64, prot: u64, flags: u64) -> u64 {
    if !sched.self_owns() {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_VFORK)
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    let i = tree.anon(n, 0, 0)
    if i == tree.NONE {
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    let va = place(a, pml4, addr, n, flags, 1)
    if failed(va) {
        tree.hold(i)
        tree.release(i) // its only holder gone: the inode goes
        return va
    }
    let _m = map_new(a, va, va + n, i, 0, (prot & PROT_KNOWN) | M_SHARED | M_MAYWRITE)
    va
}

/// px62: `mmap(addr, len, prot, flags, fd, off)` of a file of the tree
/// (inode `ino`; kernel/files has checked the descriptor and says what
/// it allows, `access`: ACC_READ, ACC_WRITE, ACC_APPEND; kernel/user has
/// checked `off` is on a page): the address, or -errno. A record, no
/// page filled until it is touched (`fault`). -EINVAL for a length of 0,
/// a protection or a type that is none; -EACCES for a descriptor not
/// open for reading, and for MAP_SHARED with PROT_WRITE of one not open
/// for writing, or open for appending; -EOVERFLOW when the offset and
/// the length pass what a file offset holds.
pub fn mmap_file(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, len: u64, prot: u64, flags: u64, ino: u64, off: u64, access: u64) -> u64 {
    let kind = flags & MAP_TYPE
    if (kind != MAP_PRIVATE && kind != MAP_SHARED && kind != MAP_SHARED_VALIDATE) || len == 0 || prot & PROT_BAD != 0 {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if kind == MAP_SHARED_VALIDATE && flags & MAP_SYNC != 0 {
        return NEG_EOPNOTSUPP
    }
    let is_shared = kind != MAP_PRIVATE
    if access & ACC_READ == 0 {
        return NEG_EACCES
    }
    if is_shared && prot & PROT_WRITE != 0 && (access & ACC_WRITE == 0 || access & ACC_APPEND != 0) {
        return NEG_EACCES
    }
    if len > paging.USER_TOP {
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    let n = page_up(len)
    if off > 0x7fffffffffffffff - n {
        return NEG_EOVERFLOW
    }
    if !sched.self_owns() {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_VFORK)
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    let va = place(a, pml4, addr, n, flags, 1)
    if failed(va) {
        return va
    }
    var fl = prot & PROT_KNOWN
    if is_shared {
        fl = fl | M_SHARED
        if access & ACC_WRITE != 0 && access & ACC_APPEND == 0 {
            fl = fl | M_MAYWRITE
        }
    }
    let _m = map_new(a, va, va + n, ino, off, fl)
    va
}

/// `munmap(addr, len)`: 0 or -EINVAL.
pub fn munmap(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, len: u64) -> u64 {
    if addr % PAGE != 0 || len == 0 || addr >= paging.USER_TOP || len > paging.USER_TOP - addr {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    let n = page_up(len)
    let end = if n > paging.USER_TOP - addr { paging.USER_TOP } else { addr + n }
    if !splits_ok(a, addr, end, 0) {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_RECORDS) // px62: a record cut in two needs one more
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    unmap_range(pml4, addr, end)
    unreserve(a, addr, end) // px26
    unmap_records(a, addr, end) // px62
    0
}

/// `mprotect(addr, len, prot)`: 0, -EINVAL, or -ENOMEM when a page of
/// the range is neither mapped nor reserved (nothing changed then).
/// px26: a reserved page given any access is backed with a zeroed frame
/// here (-ENOMEM when the frames run out, the pages before it backed).
pub fn mprotect(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, len: u64, prot: u64) -> u64 {
    if addr % PAGE != 0 || prot & PROT_BAD != 0 {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if len == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    if addr >= paging.USER_TOP || len > paging.USER_TOP - addr {
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    let end = page_up(addr + len)
    var pg = addr
    while pg < end {
        if !covered(a, pml4, pg) {
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    // px62: a shared mapping whose descriptor was not open for writing
    // is not made writable (-EACCES, nothing changed); a record cut at
    // the range's ends needs a free record for each cut.
    if prot & PROT_WRITE != 0 {
        var q: u64 = 0
        while q < MAPS {
            let fw = mw(a, q, M_FL)
            if mw(a, q, M_LO) != 0 && addr < mw(a, q, M_HI) && end > mw(a, q, M_LO) && fw & M_SHARED != 0 && fw & M_MAYWRITE == 0 {
                return NEG_EACCES
            }
            q += 1
        }
    }
    if edges(a, addr, end) > free_records(a) {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_RECORDS)
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    split_at(a, addr)
    split_at(a, end)
    var r: u64 = 0
    while r < MAPS {
        let lo = mw(a, r, M_LO)
        if lo != 0 && lo >= addr && mw(a, r, M_HI) <= end {
            put_mw(a, r, M_FL, (mw(a, r, M_FL) & !PROT_KNOWN) | (prot & PROT_KNOWN))
        }
        r += 1
    }
    var fl = leaf(prot)
    if prot & PROT_KNOWN != 0 {
        fl = fl | paging.US
    }
    // px34: in a range backed on touch, PROT_NONE makes the part an
    // ordinary reservation (it faults for good) and read-write leaves the
    // untouched pages to be backed when touched.
    var any_lazy = false
    pg = addr
    while pg < end && !any_lazy {
        any_lazy = mapped(pml4, pg) == 0 && lazy_at(a, pg)
        pg += PAGE
    }
    if any_lazy && prot & PROT_KNOWN == 0 {
        unreserve(a, addr, end)
        let _r = reserve(a, addr, end)
    }
    let keep_lazy = any_lazy && prot & PROT_KNOWN == PROT_RW && prot & PROT_EXEC == 0
    pg = addr
    while pg < end {
        let rec = map_at(a, pg)
        if rec != MAPS {
            // px62: a record's page is filled at its touch; a present one
            // takes the leaf its record's flags give it (`rec_leaf`).
            let e = paging.leaf(pml4, pg)
            if e != 0 {
                let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, rec_leaf(mw(a, rec, M_FL), e))
            }
            pg += PAGE
            continue
        }
        if mapped(pml4, pg) == 0 {
            if prot & PROT_KNOWN != 0 && !(keep_lazy && lazy_at(a, pg)) {
                if !map_zero(pml4, pg, leaf(prot)) {
                    put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_NO_FRAMES)
                    return NEG_ENOMEM
                }
            }
        } else {
            let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, fl)
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    0
}

/// `madvise(addr, len, advice)` (px26; the module's header says what
/// each advice does here).
pub fn madvise(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, len: u64, advice: u64) -> u64 {
    let adv = advice & 0xffffffff
    let hint = adv <= 3 || (adv >= 14 && adv <= 17)
    if !hint && adv != MADV_DONTNEED && adv != MADV_FREE && adv != MADV_GUARD_INSTALL && adv != MADV_GUARD_REMOVE {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_ADVICE)
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if addr % PAGE != 0 || addr >= paging.USER_TOP || len > paging.USER_TOP - addr {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if len == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let end = page_up(addr + len)
    var pg = addr
    while pg < end {
        if !covered(a, pml4, pg) {
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    if hint {
        return 0
    }
    pg = addr
    while pg < end {
        let rec = map_at(a, pg)
        if rec != MAPS {
            // px62: a record's page is never zeroed in place (it may be
            // the file's): MADV_DONTNEED unmaps it, so its next touch
            // reads the file again (a private copy is lost, as on
            // Linux); a guard takes U/S away from its record's leaf and
            // gives it back. MADV_FREE is a hint here.
            let e = paging.leaf(pml4, pg)
            if e != 0 {
                if adv == MADV_DONTNEED {
                    drop_page(pml4, pg)
                } else if adv == MADV_GUARD_INSTALL {
                    let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, rec_leaf(mw(a, rec, M_FL), e) & !paging.US)
                } else if adv == MADV_GUARD_REMOVE {
                    let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, rec_leaf(mw(a, rec, M_FL), e))
                }
            }
            pg += PAGE
            continue
        }
        let pa = mapped(pml4, pg)
        if pa != 0 && (adv == MADV_DONTNEED || adv == MADV_FREE) && lazy_at(a, pg) {
            // px34: a page of a range backed on touch gives its frame
            // back; its next touch backs a zeroed one.
            let f = paging.unmap(pml4, pg) else 0
            if f != 0 {
                frames.free(f)
            }
        } else if pa != 0 && adv != MADV_FREE { // MADV_FREE elsewhere: a hint, the page keeps its frame
            if adv == MADV_DONTNEED {
                let v = (pa & !(PAGE - 1)) + frames.hhdm()
                var i: u64 = 0
                while i < PAGE / 8 {
                    // # Safety: the frame behind the caller's own page,
                    // through the HHDM.
                    unsafe { ((v + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(0) }
                    i += 1
                }
            } else if adv == MADV_GUARD_INSTALL {
                let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, paging.RW | paging.NX) // no U/S: a touch faults
            } else {
                let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, paging.RW | paging.NX | paging.US)
            }
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    0
}

// --- px62: the records of files' and shared memory's mappings ---------------------

fn mw(a: u64, r: u64, w: u64) -> u64 {
    get(a, A_MAPS + r * M_WORDS + w)
}

fn put_mw(a: u64, r: u64, w: u64, v: u64) {
    put(a, A_MAPS + r * M_WORDS + w, v)
}

/// The record user page `pg` lies in, or MAPS.
fn map_at(a: u64, pg: u64) -> u64 {
    let top = get(a, A_MAPS_TOP)
    var r: u64 = 0
    while r < top {
        let lo = mw(a, r, M_LO)
        if lo != 0 && pg >= lo && pg < mw(a, r, M_HI) {
            return r
        }
        r += 1
    }
    MAPS
}

/// How many records are free.
fn free_records(a: u64) -> u64 {
    var n: u64 = 0
    var r: u64 = 0
    while r < MAPS {
        if mw(a, r, M_LO) == 0 {
            n += 1
        }
        r += 1
    }
    n
}

/// A record of [lo, hi) mapping inode `ino` from offset `off` with flags
/// `fl`, holding the inode: false when none is free (the callers have
/// counted first).
fn map_new(a: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64, ino: u64, off: u64, fl: u64) -> bool {
    var r: u64 = 0
    while r < MAPS {
        if mw(a, r, M_LO) == 0 {
            put_mw(a, r, M_LO, lo)
            put_mw(a, r, M_HI, hi)
            put_mw(a, r, M_INO, ino)
            put_mw(a, r, M_OFF, off)
            put_mw(a, r, M_FL, fl)
            tree.hold(ino)
            if r + 1 > get(a, A_MAPS_TOP) {
                put(a, A_MAPS_TOP, r + 1)
            }
            return true
        }
        r += 1
    }
    false
}

/// Record `r` goes, and its hold on its inode (the last hold of one with
/// no name gives its frames back: `tree.release`).
fn map_free(a: u64, r: u64) {
    let i = mw(a, r, M_INO)
    var w: u64 = 0
    while w < M_WORDS {
        put_mw(a, r, w, 0)
        w += 1
    }
    tree.release(i)
}

/// How many records a cut of [lo, hi) out of every record makes: one for
/// each record [lo, hi) lies strictly inside.
fn splits(a: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64) -> u64 {
    var n: u64 = 0
    var r: u64 = 0
    while r < MAPS {
        let rl = mw(a, r, M_LO)
        if rl != 0 && rl < lo && mw(a, r, M_HI) > hi {
            n += 1
        }
        r += 1
    }
    n
}

/// Whether the records free cover a cut of [lo, hi) and `extra` more.
fn splits_ok(a: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64, extra: u64) -> bool {
    splits(a, lo, hi) + extra <= free_records(a)
}

/// How many records splitting every record at `lo` and at `hi` makes
/// (`mprotect`: a record crossing an end is cut there).
fn edges(a: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64) -> u64 {
    var n: u64 = 0
    var r: u64 = 0
    while r < MAPS {
        let rl = mw(a, r, M_LO)
        let rh = mw(a, r, M_HI)
        if rl != 0 && rl < lo && rh > lo {
            n += 1
        }
        if rl != 0 && rl < hi && rh > hi {
            n += 1
        }
        r += 1
    }
    n
}

/// The record `x` lies strictly inside becomes two, cut at `x` (the
/// second holding the inode again); nothing when none.
fn split_at(a: u64, x: u64) {
    var r: u64 = 0
    while r < MAPS {
        let rl = mw(a, r, M_LO)
        let rh = mw(a, r, M_HI)
        if rl != 0 && rl < x && rh > x {
            put_mw(a, r, M_HI, x)
            let _m = map_new(a, x, rh, mw(a, r, M_INO), mw(a, r, M_OFF) + (x - rl), mw(a, r, M_FL))
            return
        }
        r += 1
    }
}

/// Take [lo, hi) out of every record, as `unreserve` does a reservation:
/// a record inside it goes (its hold too), one over an end is cut back
/// (its offset moved with its first byte), one around it is split (the
/// callers have counted a free record for that).
fn unmap_records(a: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64) {
    var r: u64 = 0
    while r < MAPS {
        let rl = mw(a, r, M_LO)
        let rh = mw(a, r, M_HI)
        if rl != 0 && lo < rh && hi > rl {
            if lo <= rl && hi >= rh {
                map_free(a, r)
            } else if lo <= rl {
                put_mw(a, r, M_OFF, mw(a, r, M_OFF) + (hi - rl))
                put_mw(a, r, M_LO, hi)
            } else if hi >= rh {
                put_mw(a, r, M_HI, lo)
            } else {
                put_mw(a, r, M_HI, lo)
                let _m = map_new(a, hi, rh, mw(a, r, M_INO), mw(a, r, M_OFF) + (hi - rl), mw(a, r, M_FL))
            }
        }
        r += 1
    }
}

/// Whether user page `pg` belongs to a mapping: mapped, reserved, or in a
/// record.
fn covered(a: u64, pml4: u64, pg: u64) -> bool {
    mapped(pml4, pg) != 0 || reserved_from(a, pg) != 0 || map_at(a, pg) != MAPS
}

/// The leaf a present page of a record with flags `fl` takes, given its
/// leaf `e` now: NX without PROT_EXEC, U/S with any access; a file's
/// frame stays BORROWED, read-write in a shared mapping that may be
/// written, read-only and COW in a private one; a private page of its
/// own read-write when the mapping may be written.
fn rec_leaf(fl: u64, e: u64) -> u64 {
    let prot = fl & PROT_KNOWN
    var f: u64 = if prot & PROT_EXEC == 0 { paging.NX } else { 0 }
    if prot != 0 {
        f = f | paging.US
    }
    if e & paging.BORROWED != 0 {
        f = f | paging.BORROWED
        if prot & PROT_WRITE != 0 {
            f = f | (if fl & M_SHARED != 0 { paging.RW } else { paging.COW })
        }
    } else if prot & PROT_WRITE != 0 {
        f = f | paging.RW
    }
    f
}

fn put_leaf(pml4: u64, pg: u64, pa: u64, f: u64) -> bool {
    let r = paging.map_user(pml4, pg, pa, f) else 99
    r != 99
}

/// A touch of user page `pg` of the space at `pml4` that faulted (or
/// that kernel/uaccess is about to make), a write when `write`: F_OK
/// when the page is backed now (a page of a range backed on touch, px34;
/// a record's page filled from its file, or a private page copied at
/// its first write), F_BUS for a record's page at or past its file's
/// end (or no frame to fill it with), F_NO when it is the program's to
/// die of (SIGSEGV). The module's header says how a record's page is
/// filled.
pub fn fault(a: u64, pml4: u64, pg: u64, write: bool) -> u64 {
    if pg >= paging.USER_TOP {
        return F_NO
    }
    let e = paging.leaf(pml4, pg)
    let r = map_at(a, pg)
    if r == MAPS {
        if e == 0 && back(a, pml4, pg) {
            return F_OK
        }
        return F_NO
    }
    let fl = mw(a, r, M_FL)
    let prot = fl & PROT_KNOWN
    if prot == 0 || (write && prot & PROT_WRITE == 0) {
        return F_NO
    }
    if e != 0 {
        if write && e & paging.COW != 0 {
            return if unshare(pml4, pg, e) { F_OK } else { F_BUS }
        }
        return F_NO // a present page the access is refused: the mapping's protection
    }
    let ino = mw(a, r, M_INO)
    let off = mw(a, r, M_OFF) + (pg - mw(a, r, M_LO))
    if off >= tree.bytes(ino) {
        return F_BUS // the page is past the file's end (BUS_ADRERR)
    }
    let idx = off / PAGE
    let nx = if prot & PROT_EXEC == 0 { paging.NX } else { 0 }
    let is_shared = fl & M_SHARED != 0
    if is_shared || !write {
        let pa = tree.page(ino, idx)
        if pa != 0 {
            var f = nx | paging.BORROWED
            if prot & PROT_WRITE != 0 {
                f = f | (if is_shared { paging.RW } else { paging.COW })
            }
            return if put_leaf(pml4, pg, pa, f) { F_OK } else { F_BUS }
        }
        if is_shared {
            return F_BUS // the tree at its cap: no frame for the page (tmpfs's SIGBUS)
        }
    }
    // A private page of its own: the first write, or a read with the
    // tree at its cap.
    let pa = frames.alloc() else 0
    if pa == 0 {
        return F_BUS
    }
    tree.read_page(ino, idx, pa + frames.hhdm())
    let f = nx | (if prot & PROT_WRITE != 0 { paging.RW } else { 0 })
    if !put_leaf(pml4, pg, pa, f) {
        frames.free(pa)
        return F_BUS
    }
    F_OK
}

/// The first write to a private mapping's page shared with its file (a
/// COW leaf `e`): the page copied into a frame of the process's own,
/// mapped read-write in its place; the file's frame stays the file's.
/// False with no frame left.
fn unshare(pml4: u64, pg: u64, e: u64) -> bool {
    let pa = frames.alloc() else 0
    if pa == 0 {
        return false
    }
    let h = frames.hhdm()
    let from = (e & ADDR) + h
    let to = pa + h
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < PAGE {
        // # Safety: two frames through the HHDM: the file's page (present
        // in the caller's space) and the one just taken.
        unsafe { ((to + k) as *u64).write_volatile(((from + k) as *u64).read_volatile()) }
        k += 8
    }
    let _old = paging.unmap(pml4, pg) else 0
    if !put_leaf(pml4, pg, pa, (e & paging.NX) | paging.RW) {
        frames.free(pa)
        return false
    }
    true
}

/// A fork's child (area `a`, a copy of the parent's) holds every
/// record's file again; its mremap census starts at nothing.
pub fn inherit(a: u64) {
    var r: u64 = 0
    while r < MAPS {
        if mw(a, r, M_LO) != 0 {
            tree.hold(mw(a, r, M_INO))
        }
        r += 1
    }
    put(a, A_REMAP, 0)
    put(a, A_REMAP + 1, 0)
    put(a, A_REMAP + 2, 0)
}

/// Every record goes, and its hold on its file (`execve`, through
/// `start`; the end of a process, through kernel/files' `close_all`).
/// The pages stay mapped until the space itself goes; a borrowed one is
/// never freed with it (kernel/paging's `free_user`).
pub fn drop_all(a: u64) {
    var r: u64 = 0
    while r < MAPS {
        if mw(a, r, M_LO) != 0 {
            map_free(a, r)
        }
        r += 1
    }
    put(a, A_MAPS_TOP, 0)
    put(a, A_REMAP, 0)
    put(a, A_REMAP + 1, 0)
    put(a, A_REMAP + 2, 0)
}

/// File `ino` is about to be cut to `size` bytes (kernel/files, before
/// kernel/tree gives the frames past it back): in every process, every
/// page of a record of it at or past the new end is unmapped — its own
/// copy freed, a borrowed frame left to the file — so its next touch is
/// SIGBUS (measured: private copies too) and no space keeps a frame the
/// tree has given back.
pub fn cut_maps(ino: u64, size: u64) {
    let keep = page_up(size)
    var s: u64 = 0
    while s < sched.SLOTS {
        let (a, pml4) = sched.space_of(s)
        if a != 0 && pml4 != 0 {
            var r: u64 = 0
            while r < MAPS {
                let lo = mw(a, r, M_LO)
                if lo != 0 && mw(a, r, M_INO) == ino {
                    let hi = mw(a, r, M_HI)
                    let off = mw(a, r, M_OFF)
                    var pg = lo
                    while pg < hi {
                        if off + (pg - lo) >= keep {
                            drop_page(pml4, pg)
                        }
                        pg += PAGE
                    }
                }
                r += 1
            }
        }
        s += 1
    }
}

/// mremap's census for kernel/user's line at a process's end: (calls
/// served, grown in place, moved).
pub fn remap_census(a: u64) -> (u64, u64, u64) {
    (get(a, A_REMAP), get(a, A_REMAP + 1), get(a, A_REMAP + 2))
}

fn count(a: u64, w: u64) {
    put(a, A_REMAP + w, get(a, A_REMAP + w) + 1)
}

// --- px62: mremap (pax#127) --------------------------------------------------------

const K_NONE: u64 = 0
const K_PLAIN: u64 = 1 // present anonymous pages with one leaf's flags
const K_RESV: u64 = 2 // inside one reservation
const K_REC: u64 = 3 // inside one record

/// The reservation entry user page `pg` lies in, or RESV.
fn resv_at(a: u64, pg: u64) -> u64 {
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV {
        let lo = get(a, A_RESV + i * 2)
        if lo != 0 && pg >= lo && pg < hi_of(a, i) {
            return i
        }
        i += 1
    }
    RESV
}

fn resv_free(a: u64) -> u64 {
    var n: u64 = 0
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < RESV {
        if get(a, A_RESV + i * 2) == 0 {
            n += 1
        }
        i += 1
    }
    n
}

/// What mapping [old, old + n) is: (K_REC and the record, K_RESV and the
/// entry, K_PLAIN and the leaf's flags, or K_NONE: no one mapping).
fn classify(a: u64, pml4: u64, old: u64, n: u64) -> (u64, u64, u64) {
    let r = map_at(a, old)
    if r != MAPS {
        if old + n <= mw(a, r, M_HI) {
            return (K_REC, r, 0)
        }
        return (K_NONE, 0, 0)
    }
    let q = resv_at(a, old)
    if q != RESV {
        if old + n <= hi_of(a, q) {
            return (K_RESV, q, 0)
        }
        return (K_NONE, 0, 0)
    }
    let e0 = paging.leaf(pml4, old)
    if e0 == 0 || e0 & paging.BORROWED != 0 {
        return (K_NONE, 0, 0)
    }
    let want = e0 & (paging.RW | paging.US | paging.NX)
    var pg = old + PAGE
    while pg < old + n {
        let e = paging.leaf(pml4, pg)
        if e == 0 || e & (paging.RW | paging.US | paging.NX | paging.BORROWED) != want || map_at(a, pg) != MAPS || resv_at(a, pg) != RESV {
            return (K_NONE, 0, 0)
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    (K_PLAIN, 0, want)
}

/// A zeroed frame at user page `pg` with the leaf flags `lf` (U/S taken
/// away when `lf` has none: a PROT_NONE page made the old way).
fn map_like(pml4: u64, pg: u64, lf: u64) -> bool {
    if !map_zero(pml4, pg, lf & (paging.RW | paging.NX)) {
        return false
    }
    if lf & paging.US == 0 {
        let _ok = paging.protect_user(pml4, pg, lf)
    }
    true
}

/// Zeroed frames for [lo, hi) with the flags `lf`, all or none.
fn map_run(pml4: u64, lo: u64, hi: u64, lf: u64) -> bool {
    var pg = lo
    while pg < hi {
        if !map_like(pml4, pg, lf) {
            unmap_range(pml4, lo, pg)
            return false
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    true
}

/// Move every present page of [from, from + n) to [to, to + n): its leaf
/// moves, no byte is copied. False when a table could not be made (what
/// moved is put back).
fn move_pages(pml4: u64, from: u64, to: u64, n: u64) -> bool {
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < n {
        let e = paging.leaf(pml4, from + k)
        if e != 0 {
            let pa = paging.unmap(pml4, from + k) else 0
            let lf = e & (paging.RW | paging.US | paging.NX | paging.BORROWED | paging.COW)
            if !put_leaf(pml4, to + k, pa, lf & !paging.US) {
                let _back = put_leaf(pml4, from + k, pa, lf & !paging.US)
                let _p = paging.protect_user(pml4, from + k, lf)
                var j: u64 = 0
                while j < k {
                    let ej = paging.leaf(pml4, to + j)
                    if ej != 0 {
                        let pj = paging.unmap(pml4, to + j) else 0
                        let fj = ej & (paging.RW | paging.US | paging.NX | paging.BORROWED | paging.COW)
                        let _b = put_leaf(pml4, from + j, pj, fj & !paging.US)
                        let _q = paging.protect_user(pml4, from + j, fj)
                    }
                    j += PAGE
                }
                return false
            }
            let _p = paging.protect_user(pml4, to + k, lf)
        }
        k += PAGE
    }
    true
}

/// `mremap(old, old_size, new_size, flags, new_address)`: the mapping's
/// address, or -errno (the module's header says what is served and what
/// is refused; new_address is MREMAP_FIXED's alone, and that is refused,
/// so it is not read).
pub fn mremap(a: u64, pml4: u64, old: u64, osz: u64, nsz: u64, flags: u64) -> u64 {
    if flags & !(MREMAP_MAYMOVE | MREMAP_FIXED | MREMAP_DONTUNMAP) != 0 {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if flags & (MREMAP_FIXED | MREMAP_DONTUNMAP) != 0 && flags & MREMAP_MAYMOVE == 0 {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if old % PAGE != 0 || nsz == 0 || nsz > paging.USER_TOP || osz > paging.USER_TOP {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if flags & MREMAP_FIXED != 0 {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_REMAP_FIXED) // R-px62-2
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if flags & MREMAP_DONTUNMAP != 0 {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_DONTUNMAP)
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    let os = page_up(osz)
    let ns = page_up(nsz)
    if old >= paging.USER_TOP {
        return NEG_EFAULT
    }
    if os == 0 {
        let r0 = map_at(a, old)
        if r0 != MAPS && mw(a, r0, M_FL) & M_SHARED != 0 && flags & MREMAP_MAYMOVE != 0 {
            put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_DUP)
        }
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if os > paging.USER_TOP - old {
        return NEG_EFAULT
    }
    let (kind, which, lf) = classify(a, pml4, old, os)
    if kind == K_NONE {
        return NEG_EFAULT
    }
    if ns == os {
        count(a, 0)
        return old
    }
    if ns < os {
        if !splits_ok(a, old + ns, old + os, 0) {
            put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_RECORDS)
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
        unmap_range(pml4, old + ns, old + os)
        unreserve(a, old + ns, old + os)
        unmap_records(a, old + ns, old + os)
        count(a, 0)
        return old
    }
    // Larger: in place when every page up to the new size is free.
    var room = ns <= paging.USER_TOP - old
    var pg = old + os
    while room && pg < old + ns {
        if taken(a, pml4, pg) {
            room = false
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    if room {
        if kind == K_PLAIN {
            if !map_run(pml4, old + os, old + ns, lf) {
                put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_NO_FRAMES)
                return NEG_ENOMEM
            }
        } else if kind == K_RESV {
            put(a, A_RESV + which * 2 + 1, (old + ns) | lazy_of(a, which))
        } else {
            put_mw(a, which, M_HI, old + ns)
        }
        count(a, 0)
        count(a, 1)
        return old
    }
    if flags & MREMAP_MAYMOVE == 0 {
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    // Moved, to where `pick` chooses.
    if kind == K_REC && !splits_ok(a, old, old + os, 1) {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_RECORDS)
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    if kind == K_RESV {
        let rl = get(a, A_RESV + which * 2)
        let rh = hi_of(a, which)
        var need: u64 = 1
        if rl < old && rh > old + os {
            need = 2
        } else if rl == old && rh == old + os {
            need = 0
        }
        if resv_free(a) < need {
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
    }
    let va = pick(a, pml4, ns)
    if va == 0 {
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    if kind == K_PLAIN && !map_run(pml4, va + os, va + ns, lf) {
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_NO_FRAMES)
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    if !move_pages(pml4, old, va, os) {
        if kind == K_PLAIN {
            unmap_range(pml4, va + os, va + ns)
        }
        put(a, A_REFUSAL, R_NO_FRAMES)
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    if kind == K_RESV {
        let k = lazy_of(a, which)
        unreserve(a, old, old + os)
        let _r = reserve_kind(a, va, va + ns, k)
    } else if kind == K_REC {
        let ino = mw(a, which, M_INO)
        let off = mw(a, which, M_OFF) + (old - mw(a, which, M_LO))
        let fl = mw(a, which, M_FL)
        tree.hold(ino) // while the old record is cut
        unmap_records(a, old, old + os)
        let _m = map_new(a, va, va + ns, ino, off, fl)
        tree.release(ino)
    }
    count(a, 0)
    count(a, 2)
    va
}

// --- px62: msync -------------------------------------------------------------------

/// `msync(addr, len, flags)`: 0, or -EINVAL, -ENOMEM (the module's
/// header says why there is nothing to write).
pub fn msync(a: u64, pml4: u64, addr: u64, len: u64, flags: u64) -> u64 {
    let fl = flags & 0xffffffff
    if fl & !(MS_ASYNC | MS_INVALIDATE | MS_SYNC) != 0 || (fl & MS_ASYNC != 0 && fl & MS_SYNC != 0) {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if addr % PAGE != 0 {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    if len == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    if addr >= paging.USER_TOP || len > paging.USER_TOP - addr {
        return NEG_ENOMEM
    }
    let end = page_up(addr + len)
    var pg = addr
    while pg < end {
        if !covered(a, pml4, pg) {
            return NEG_ENOMEM
        }
        pg += PAGE
    }
    0
}
